This version keeps the recursion very direct. At each position, one branch skips the value and the other branch adds it. Once the index reaches the end, the current list is one complete subset.

The empty input is handled by the base case, so the result still contains one empty vector. The input vector stays unchanged.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int> > powerSet(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<vector<int> > result;
        vector<int> current;
        generate(nums, 0, current, result);
        return result;
    }

private:
    void generate(vector<int>& nums, int index, vector<int>& current,
                  vector<vector<int> >& result) {
        if (index == nums.size()) {
            result.push_back(current);
            return;
        }

        generate(nums, index + 1, current, result);

        current.push_back(nums[index]);
        generate(nums, index + 1, current, result);
        current.pop_back();
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n * 2^n)`, since there are `2^n` leaves and each stored subset may contain `n` values.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion stack and current subset, excluding the output.

Backtracking works by making a choice for each number: either include it in the current subset or skip it. When every number has been checked, the current subset is added to the answer.

A dry run for `[1, 2]` visits these completed choices: `[]`, `[2]`, `[1]`, and `[1, 2]`. The remove call restores the list before trying the next choice.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> powerSet(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        buildSubsets(nums, 0, new ArrayList<>(), result);
        return result;
    }

    private void buildSubsets(int[] nums, int index,
                              List<Integer> current,
                              List<List<Integer>> result) {
        if (index == nums.length) {
            result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
            return;
        }

        buildSubsets(nums, index + 1, current, result);

        current.add(nums[index]);
        buildSubsets(nums, index + 1, current, result);
        current.remove(current.size() - 1);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n * 2^n)` including the time needed to copy each finished subset.

Space complexity: `O(n)` recursion and temporary list space, not counting the returned result. For an empty array, the method correctly returns `[[]]`.

I used the simple list-building method here. Start with one empty subset. For every number, copy all subsets made so far and add that number to each copy.

For example, with `[1, 2]`, the list grows from `[[]]` to `[[], [1]]`, then to `[[], [1], [2], [1, 2]]`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int> > powerSet(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<vector<int> > result(1);

        for (int number : nums) {
            int currentSize = result.size();
            for (int i = 0; i < currentSize; ++i) {
                vector<int> nextSubset = result[i];
                nextSubset.push_back(number);
                result.push_back(nextSubset);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n * 2^n)`, because there are `2^n` subsets and copying a subset can take up to `O(n)` time.

Space complexity: `O(n * 2^n)` for the returned subsets. The input array is not changed.

Here the answer grows in rounds. Start with the empty list. For each number, make a new group by adding that number to every existing subset, then append the new group.

A quick dry run with `[1, 2]` starts as `[[]]`. After `1` it is `[[], [1]]`; after `2` the new copies are `[2]` and `[1, 2]`.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> powerSet(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        result.add(new ArrayList<>());

        for (int number : nums) {
            int oldSize = result.size();
            for (int i = 0; i < oldSize; i++) {
                List<Integer> extended = new ArrayList<>(result.get(i));
                extended.add(number);
                result.add(extended);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n * 2^n)` because each round copies the existing subsets.

Space complexity: `O(n * 2^n)` for the returned subsets. This also works when `nums` is empty, returning a list containing only an empty list.

## Intuition

For every element in the array, we have only **two choices**:

* Include the element in the current subset
* Exclude the element from the current subset

By making these two decisions for each element, we can generate **all possible subsets**. This is why recursion works perfectly for the power set problem.

---

## Approach

We use **recursion with backtracking** to solve the problem.

* Create a helper function that takes the current index and a temporary list `output`.
* If the index reaches the end of the array, store the current subset in the answer.
* Otherwise:

  * First, **include** the current element and move to the next index.
  * Then, **remove** the element (backtrack) and try the **exclude** case.

This way, every subset is explored exactly once.

---

## Solution

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void solve(vector<int> &nums, vector<vector<int>> &ans,
               vector<int> &output, int index) {
        if (index == nums.size()) {
            ans.push_back(output);
            return;
        }

        // Include current element
        output.push_back(nums[index]);
        solve(nums, ans, output, index + 1);

        // Exclude current element (backtracking)
        output.pop_back();
        solve(nums, ans, output, index + 1);
    }

    vector<vector<int>> powerSet(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<vector<int>> ans;
        vector<int> output;
        solve(nums, ans, output, 0);
        return ans;
    }
};
```

---

## Complexity

**Time Complexity:** `O(2^n)`
Each element has two choices: include or exclude.

**Space Complexity:** `O(n)`
Used by the recursion stack and the temporary subset.

---

### Final Note

This is a classic **include–exclude recursion pattern**. Once you understand this, many recursion problems become easy.
